fxfile
======

Copyright (c) 2013 Leon Lee Author. All rights reserved.

Overview
--------
fxfile (formerly flyExplorer) is a free, open source file manager for Microsoft Windows.

Modern Build Environment
------------------------
The default build path targets Visual Studio 2026 and the v145 platform toolset.

Required software:

1. Visual Studio 2026
   - Install the "Desktop development with C++" workload.
   - Install MFC for the current MSVC v145 x64/x86 build tools.
   - Install a current Windows 10/11 SDK.
2. Python 3.9 or newer.
3. Git.

The old Python 2.7.4 and Visual Studio 2015/v140 toolchain are no longer required
for the default build path.

Development Policy and Fast Preflight
-------------------------------------
The repository vendors the validated `universal-agent-docs` policy bundle under
`.agent-policy/`. Project-specific instructions remain in `AGENTS.md` and
project facts in `.agent-policy/PROJECT.md`.

Before a C++ checkpoint, run:

    python build/validate_source_invariants.py

`build.bat` runs this preflight automatically before downloading tools,
deleting stale generated projects, or running GYP. The current preflight checks
direct header dependencies for cross-cutting ModernUI symbols and verifies the
DPI/manifest integration points. It is a static check and does not replace a
Visual Studio compile/link build.

Policy/readiness checks can be run with:

    python -m pip install --require-hashes --requirement .agent-policy/requirements.lock
    python .agent-policy/scripts/validate.py
    python .agent-policy/scripts/validate.py --project-root . --readiness development
    python .agent-policy/scripts/conformance.py

These checks are also wired to repository CI. A policy/static PASS must not be
reported as a product compile, integration, or runtime PASS.

Windows Product Build CI
------------------------
The separate `.github/workflows/windows-build.yml` workflow runs on GitHub's
Windows Server 2025 image with Visual Studio 2026. Its x64 job regenerates the
solution with `build.bat 2026 x64`, verifies the v145/MFC build contract,
builds `Release-x64-Unicode | x64`, and checks the main executable plus the
expected x64 and Win32 helper artifacts.

The same workflow also regenerates the x86 solution with `build.bat 2026 x86`
and builds both `Release-x86-Unicode | Win32` and
`Release-x86-Multibyte | Win32`. The Unicode build verifies the
`libxprw.dll/.lib` contract and intentionally excludes the multibyte updater.
The multibyte build verifies `libxpr.dll/.lib`, builds
`fxfile-upchecker.exe`, checks its packaged `upchecker` runtime directory,
and validates the current distribution input names. All product configurations
fail CI if compiler, linker, or MSBuild warnings are emitted.

After each verified release build, the workflow also runs
`build/runtime_smoke.ps1` against a temporary copy of the staged runtime. The
smoke test uses an isolated `--conf_dir`, disables launcher/update startup
behavior, starts `fxfile.exe`, requires a top-level main window, checks that
the process remains alive for a short stability window, and then cleans up its
temporary files and test registry state. This hosted-runner launch smoke runs
for x64 Unicode, x86 Unicode, and x86 Multibyte.

A successful Windows Product Build is compile/link, warning, build-contract,
artifact, and **hosted-runner launch** evidence. GitHub's Windows runner already
contains Visual Studio and other system software, so this is not clean-Windows
Sandbox/VM evidence and does not replace interactive functional or visual
regression testing.

Hosted runtime smoke can also be invoked manually after a build, for example:

    pwsh -File build\runtime_smoke.ps1 -SourceDir bin\x64 -Configuration Release-x64-Unicode

Manual Runtime Smoke Checklist
------------------------------
For an interactive Windows or Sandbox check, keep destructive file operations
out of scope and verify at least:

1. Startup: the main window appears and no crash/missing-DLL dialog is shown.
2. Folder tree: expand Desktop, select a drive, and navigate to a test folder.
3. File list: enter a child folder and navigate back to its parent.
4. AddressBar: confirm the current path, type a test path, press Enter, open the
   dropdown, and confirm basic autocomplete behavior.
5. Tabs: create, switch, and close a tab.
6. Window behavior: minimize, restore, resize, and maximize.
7. Helpers: note launcher/keyhook/updater behavior without installing updates or
   modifying shell integration.

Record Windows Sandbox/clean-VM, interactive functional, and visual results
separately from the hosted-runner launch smoke.

Windows 11 UI Modernization
---------------------------
The current UI modernization keeps the MFC/Win32 architecture while reusing the
shared `gui/ModernUI` DPI, system-typography, and system-palette foundation.
The status-bar layer now uses the system UI font, 96-DPI logical pane metrics
that are rescaled for the current monitor, flat ThemePalette-based
background/text/separators, and settings/theme/DPI refresh handling.

`ExplorerStatusBar` keeps its existing four-pane information contract and
drive-properties double-click behavior. Its drive-usage progress remains the
existing GDI `TextProgressCtrl`, but uses DPI-aware system typography,
palette-derived fill/track/text colors, bounded percentage rendering, and
DPI-scaled insets. Other StatusBar consumers share the generic typography,
palette, and DPI metric fixes without adopting Explorer-specific progress
styling.

The first dialog-specific modernization reference is `DriveDetailDlg`. It
applies the current-DPI system UI font to its labels and value fields, preserves
the existing bold percentage text in the shared `TextProgressCtrl`, refreshes
typography and custom-control appearance for settings/theme/DPI notifications,
and uses ThemePalette colors plus DPI-scaled metrics in `DrivePieCtrl`.
Drive-data queries, localized strings, icon behavior, and the dialog's existing
information structure remain unchanged.

This is a ModernUI foundation, not a claim of complete Windows 11 dark-mode
support. Dark title bars/common-control theming, icon modernization, and broader
dialog modernization beyond this first reference remain separate work. Hosted CI
verifies compile/link, warning-free builds, launch smoke, and packaging;
100/125/150/200% visual inspection, High Contrast appearance, and interactive
status-bar/DriveDetailDlg behavior still require separate manual evidence.

Generate the Visual Studio Solution
-----------------------------------
From a Command Prompt in the repository root:

    build.bat

The default is equivalent to:

    build.bat 2026 x64

On first run, build.bat automatically clones the pinned gyp-next version into:

    tools\gyp-next

gyp-next 0.22.2 supports Visual Studio 2026. The build script removes stale
generated Visual Studio projects before regeneration and verifies that the new
.vcxproj files use the v145 platform toolset. Because gyp-next no longer
converts the legacy UseOfMFC attribute, the post-generation fixer writes the
equivalent modern MSBuild UseOfMfc=Static setting into the MFC application
projects.

The root fxfile.vcxproj is an aggregate project used only to group the solution.
It now exposes the same x86/x64 solution configuration names as the real build
projects so Visual Studio can load Release-x64-Unicode and Debug-x64-Unicode
without configuration-mismatch errors.

The main application, xpr, and crash module have native x64 configurations.
The legacy updater, launcher, and keyboard-hook helper are intentionally Win32
projects. When an x64 solution is generated, build\fix_solution_configs.py
maps the launcher and keyboard hook to Win32. The updater is kept loadable but
is not built as part of an x64 solution build because it depends on the x86
multibyte xpr library. Build Release-x86-Multibyte when you need the updater
for packaging. The root aggregate project is also excluded from Build Solution
because all real projects are already listed individually.

If you previously generated fxfile.sln with Visual Studio 2015/v140 or with an
older version of this build script, simply run:

    build.bat

again. Stale generated solution/project files, including the root aggregate
fxfile.vcxproj, are removed automatically before regeneration.

After generation, open:

    fxfile.sln

Build fxfile
------------
For the recommended 64-bit release build, select:

    Configuration: Release-x64-Unicode
    Platform:      x64

Then run "Build Solution" in Visual Studio. The x64 build produces the main
application, x64 xpr/crash modules, and the Win32 launcher/keyhook helpers.
The updater is intentionally deferred; build Release-x86-Multibyte separately
when preparing a full distribution.

The main executable is generated under:

    bin\x64\fxfile.exe

Prepare Runtime Files
---------------------
build.bat now prepares the bundled runtime libraries and language-resource
directories automatically before you open the generated solution. The helper
post-build steps also create their destination directories and run non-
interactively.

You can still refresh these files manually at any time:

    copy_lib.bat
    copy_languages.bat

After a successful Release-x64-Unicode build, run:

    bin\x64\fxfile.exe

Distribution
------------
The `dist` directory contains the release staging, portable-package, and NSIS
installer scripts. A full package requires all three verified release builds:
`Release-x64-Unicode | x64`, `Release-x86-Unicode | Win32`, and
`Release-x86-Multibyte | Win32`; the multibyte build provides the Win32 update
checker that is staged under each platform's `upchecker` directory.

After those builds are available under `bin`, run from the repository root:

    dist\package.bat

Packaging uses the vendored `dist\7zip\7za.exe` and
`dist\nsis\makensis.exe`, so the package build does not download an external
NSIS toolchain. It creates:

    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev.exe
    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev_x64_portable.zip
    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev_x86_unicode_portable.zip
    dist\fxfile_v2.0.4-dev_x86_multibyte_portable.zip

`build\validate_package.ps1` validates the staged runtime trees and portable
ZIP payloads, rejects development artifacts such as PDB/OBJ/LIB/project files,
and checks that the NSIS installer was actually produced. The Windows Product
Build workflow runs this packaging path after the build/runtime jobs, writes
`SHA256SUMS.txt`, and uploads the validated outputs as the
`flyExplorer-release-package` GitHub Actions artifact.

Package Build/Payload PASS is not clean-install evidence. The CI job does not
install or uninstall the NSIS package, modify shell integration, or prove a
clean Windows Sandbox/VM installation. Those checks remain separate runtime
validation work.

Legacy build
------------
The original project used a customized fxfile/gyp fork, Python 2.7.4, and
Visual Studio 2015/v140. Those instructions are retained only in repository
history; the default build path now targets Visual Studio 2026/v145.

